<response><accesstype>CCBY - IEEE is not the copyright holder of this material. Please follow the instructions via <a class="vglnk" href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/" rel="nofollow"><span>https</span><span>://</span><span>creativecommons</span><span>.</span><span>org</span><span>/</span><span>licenses</span><span>/</span><span>by</span><span>/</span><span>4</span><span>.</span><span>0</span><span>/</span></a> to obtain full-text articles and stipulations in the API documentation.</accesstype><div id="BodyWrapper" class="ArticlePage" xmlns:ieee="http://www.ieeexplore.ieee.org"><div id="article">
    <div class="section" id="sec1"><div class="header article-hdr"><div class="kicker">
                                SECTION I.</div><h2>Introduction</h2></div><p>Brain tumors (BT) are a high-mortality public health problem that consists of heterogeneous tissues originating from different anatomical regions of the brain. Primary BT affects about 14 persons per 100,000 people annually. More common is the second BT, which puts more pressure on healthcare systems worldwide <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref1" id="context_ref_1_1" data-range="ref1">[1]</a>. BT not only influences physical health but also plays an important role in the quality of life, cognitive functions, and emotions of the patients <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref4" id="context_ref_4_1" data-range="ref4">[4]</a>, <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref5" id="context_ref_5_1" data-range="ref5">[5]</a>. The heterogeneous nature and response to the treatment of brain tumors, among other clinically relevant factors, underlines the need for advancing diagnostic and therapeutic approaches to improve patient outcomes <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref3" id="context_ref_3_1" data-range="ref3">[3]</a>. Recent major advances in medical imaging and AI have polished the way BT is identified and classified. Deep learning methods, specifically CNN, have been demonstrated to perform particularly well in estimating medical images. This has resulted in automated approaches for locating BT <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref7" id="context_ref_7_1" data-range="ref7">[7]</a>. Using large databases of annotated medical images, these models learn the nuanced associations that are associated with different classes of tumors <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref5" id="context_ref_5_1" data-range="ref5">[5]</a>, which help improve diagnostic precision and efficiency. The nature of patient information is a major roadblock for healthcare organizations to cooperate and share data. However, FL (FL) can be the solution to such problems.</p><p>Federal Learning, a distributed Machine Learning approach, permits many healthcare agencies to work together to train model while enjoying the privacy of their patientâ€™s private data. This approach provides increased data privacy without losing the ability to develop strong prediction models. For BT segmentation and classification, FL enables data from multiple medical centers to be merged without compromising individual data privacy, thus enhancing the robustness and accuracy of the model <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref6" id="context_ref_6_1" data-range="ref6">[6]</a>, <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref9" id="context_ref_9_1" data-range="ref9">[9]</a>. Jeffrey et al. <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref1" id="context_ref_1_1" data-range="ref1">[1]</a> indicated that it would be acceptable, for example, to obtain high similarity between FL and centralized learning methods while preserving patient data privacy (i.e., decentralized infrastructure), hence making FL a promising approach in medical applications. In addition, FL models are designed specifically to address the unique challenges of BT analysis, such as data heterogeneity and segmentation task requirements for high accuracy <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref8" id="context_ref_8_1" data-range="ref8">[8]</a>.</p><p>Here, <a ref-type="fig" anchor="fig1" class="fulltext-link">Figure 1</a> shows a high-level architectural overview of FL, specifically the communication flow between Clients and Central Server in terms of rounds during model training. Here is an outline of how the pieces come together:
</p><ol><li value="1" data-list-item-label="1."><p>Clients: Clients are individual devices or entities (like mobile phones, sensors, and computers) that all have their own local data. FL is a distributed data training approach that maintains the privacy of local data, where each client trains the model on its private data without directly providing it to a server.</p></li><li value="2" data-list-item-label="2."><p>Server: It is the centralized entity to manage the global model. Responsible for coordinating the training process by aggregating models after clients run their local training, updating the global model, and returning this updated model to clients.</p></li><li value="3" data-list-item-label="3."><p>Global Model: The global model held by the server. The aggregation of the locally trained models received from the clients updates this model.</p></li><li value="4" data-list-item-label="4."><p>Aggregation: It is the way the server aggregates updates sent from all clients. In the aggregation step, a weighted average or some other aggregation is calculated to obtain the updated global model using client contributions.</p></li></ol>
<div class="figure figure-full" id="fig1"><!--
          Workaround for combined images.Eg.- 1000116 Fig. 5
        --><div class="img-wrap"><a href="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/mediastore/IEEE/content/media/6287639/10820123/10918716/tejan1-3549440-large.gif" data-fig-id="fig1"><img class="document-ft-image" src="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/mediastore/IEEE/content/media/6287639/10820123/10918716/tejan1-3549440-small.gif" loading="lazy" alt="FIGURE 1. - Federated learning process flow."></a><div class="zoom-container"><button class="zoom" title="View Larger Image" aria-label="View Larger Image" data-fig-id="fig1"></button></div></div><div class="figcaption"><b class="title">FIGURE 1. </b><fig><p>Federated learning process flow.</p></fig></div><p class="links"><button ref-type="fig-link" data-docid="10918716" class="all">Show All</button></p></div><p></p><div class="section_2" id="sec1a"><h3>A. Step by Step Process</h3><p>
          </p><ol><li value="1" data-list-item-label="1."><p>Send model for training on the local data of the client: In this first step, a server would send an initial version of the global model to each client. This model is then sent to each client device (an individual phone, sensor, or computer), which locally prepares for training on the new model.</p></li><li value="2" data-list-item-label="2."><p>Local training of the model and sending it to the server: Here, every client trains the model on its local data. In contrast to this local training, instead of raw data, the clients return model updates (often the learned weights or gradients) to the server. This means that no private data will ever leave client devices, thus protecting privacy.</p></li><li value="3" data-list-item-label="3."><p>Aggregate the models received from all clients and then update the global model: The server gathers all of the client model updates. It aggregates these updates.</p></li><li value="4" data-list-item-label="4."><p>Send the updated model to clients and repeat: At the same time, once the server alters the global model, the server transmits the updated model to the clients. This process goes on locally, then aggregates and is updated globally. It goes through many cycles known as rounds of communication until the modelâ€™s performance is optimal.</p></li></ol>
        <p></p><p>This cyclic workflow permits the model to leverage multiple data sources without violating the data privacy of a particular dataset. The areas of the biggest benefit of FL are healthcare, finance, and individualized applications, as data security and privacy are highly important there. Because in FL, data are kept local on the device, and only the model updates are sent, the communication costs are also lower, besides getting more accurate models on clients with different data distribution.</p></div><div class="section_2" id="sec1b"><h3>B. Research Objective</h3><p>This research aims to:
</p><ol><li value="1" data-list-item-label="1."><p>Enhance the data confidentiality and privacy using FL to train the models with the multiple providersâ€™ data without insisting on centralizing patient data.</p></li><li value="2" data-list-item-label="2."><p>This makes use of the Federated Averaging (FedAvg) and Federated Proximal (FedProx) algorithms to address data heterogeneity issues for clients in the development of a globally efficient brain tumor classification model.</p></li><li value="3" data-list-item-label="3."><p>Moreover, it reviews the effectiveness of FedProxâ€™s proximal regularization in enhancing stability and accuracy in the case of non-IID data distribution.</p></li><li value="4" data-list-item-label="4."><p>Classify BT using CNN on decentralized data.</p></li></ol><p></p></div><div class="section_2" id="sec1c"><h3>C. Research Contributions</h3><div class="section_2" id="sec1c1"><h4>1) Introduction of A FL Framework for BTC</h4><p>Herein, this research proposes a distributed learning framework involving FedAvg and FedProx for CNN training on data distributed across multiple healthcare providers. Another requirement for medical applications is avoiding direct sharing of data, and through FL, the framework achieves data privacy to allow collaborative model training.</p></div><div class="section_2" id="sec1c2"><h4>2) Integration of Fedprox for Handling Data Heterogeneity</h4><p>Due to the fact that the distributions of data across multiple healthcare providers are inherently heterogeneous, the study uses FedProx to handle this heterogeneity. FedProx proposes to add a proximal regularization term that discourages the local model from deviating far from the global model during its training, which contributes to more robust convergence across non-IID data scenarios.</p></div><div class="section_2" id="sec1c3"><h4>3) Comprehensive Evaluation of Model Performance</h4><p>The performance of the framework is then tested by carrying out experiments with ten communication rounds. The result shows that the combined approach of FedAvg and FedPorx leads to consistent improvement in BTC.</p></div><div class="section_2" id="sec1c4"><h4>4) Preserving Data Privacy in Medical Diagnosis</h4><p>This work shows that the FL technique could promote the application of maintaining data privacy for medical diagnosis, with a capacity to seek an efficient and safe way of constructing a model cooperatively among numerous healthcare facilities. This is especially important in cases where privacy is of utmost importance, as it may be applied in medical imaging and healthcare analytics.</p></div><div class="section_2" id="sec1c5"><h4>5) Superior Performance of Global Model</h4><p>As illustrated in <a ref-type="fig" anchor="fig6" class="fulltext-link">Fig. 6</a>, the global model trained through the proposed framework outperforms the other models with a centralized learning approach, underscoring the effectiveness of FL in harnessing disparate and decentralized datasets. Our work demonstrates that in the case of non-IID data, the given framework allows patients to be classified into four classes of brain tumors. Therefore, our study has strong pragmatic significance.
</p><div class="figure figure-full" id="fig2"><!--
          Workaround for combined images.Eg.- 1000116 Fig. 5
        --><div class="img-wrap"><a href="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/mediastore/IEEE/content/media/6287639/10820123/10918716/tejan2-3549440-large.gif" data-fig-id="fig2"><img class="document-ft-image" src="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/mediastore/IEEE/content/media/6287639/10820123/10918716/tejan2-3549440-small.gif" loading="lazy" alt="FIGURE 2. - Flow of proposed methodology."></a><div class="zoom-container"><button class="zoom" title="View Larger Image" aria-label="View Larger Image" data-fig-id="fig2"></button></div></div><div class="figcaption"><b class="title">FIGURE 2. </b><fig><p>Flow of proposed methodology.</p></fig></div><p class="links"><button ref-type="fig-link" data-docid="10918716" class="all">Show All</button></p></div>
