    <div class="section" id="sec2"><div class="header article-hdr"><div class="kicker">
                                SECTION II.</div><h2>Literature Review</h2></div><p>The classification of BT is a pivotal question in medical imaging, especially because of the tumor heterogeneity, which in turn can affect the therapeutic management and the prognosis of patients. The new DL algorithms, especially CNNs, can help perform BTC from MRI data automatically. For instance, Xie et al. <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref19" id="context_ref_19_2" data-range="ref19">[19]</a> present a detailed study of CNN methodologies for BTC problems, and the issues and potential development in the field. They stress the importance of applying the principles related to such aspects as trust and transparency in the clinical uses of such models, as only a few works have considered the fact that CNNs are black boxes. To this family of works, Mohan et al. <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref18" id="context_ref_18_2" data-range="ref18">[18]</a> present a new CNN-based medical image analysis and detection network compared with VGG16 net. These reveal that deep learning models are capable of differentiating between primary brain tumors and metastases with high sensitivity and specificity. Likewise, Johnpeter and Ponnuchamy <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref15" id="context_ref_15_2" data-range="ref15">[15]</a> discussed a computer-aided detection of glioma tumors through CNN with optimific classification accuracies, further enhancing the applicability of DL in this regard. Also, an amalgam of architectures that employ different machine-learning appro- aches has been proposed. For instance, a combined feature extraction technique for BTC is described in <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref14" id="context_ref_14_2" data-range="ref14">[14]</a> using MX-ML and regularised extreme learning, which points to the possibility of optimizing the performance of classification by combining different approaches. Additionally, Azzahra <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref12" id="context_ref_12_2" data-range="ref12">[12]</a> employs Inception ResNet V2 for MRI-based BTC, with a view to applying the multi-class classification to get different types of tumors such as meningioma, glioma, and pituitary.</p><p>In the case of healthcare data privacy and sharing, it has been seen that FL is a possible paradigm. Due to this decentralized system, various hospitals can come together and train our models without any patientâ€™s personal health information being revealed. In the case of their study assessing the PySyft FL system, Budrionis et al. <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref13" id="context_ref_13_2" data-range="ref13">[13]</a> recommend its use mainly in healthcare scenarios where data centralization is not always possible. Atha <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref11" id="context_ref_11_2" data-range="ref11">[11]</a> puts forward SSBTCNet, which uses FL to improve model accuracy and protect patientsâ€™ data. The combination of FL with DL techniques has proved to enhance the BTC accuracy in image analysis. For example, Liu <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref17" id="context_ref_17_2" data-range="ref17">[17]</a> described the use of triplet contrastive learning for BTC; however, the method can be coupled with FL approaches to improve the model performance across datasets. Moreover, the results of this study reveal that more complex architectures, such as RNNs described by Kalaiselvi et al. <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref16" id="context_ref_16_2" data-range="ref16">[16]</a>, should be the target of the FL approach to improving the classification of the BT.</p><p>Furthermore, the FedProx algorithm is designed to tackle some problems of FL, especially when working with different kinds of data. This algorithm enables the incorporation of local updates at the same time as the global model; this is important given that institutional data distributions may differ greatly in the medical field. Aamir et al.â€™s <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref10" id="context_ref_10_2" data-range="ref10">[10]</a> work discusses how FedProx improves the performance of FL models for BTC while adapting to different datasets with regard to data privacy. The study done by Hossain et al. <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref29" id="context_ref_29_2" data-range="ref29">[29]</a> pays attention to the transition from binomial to multiclass classification and improves the prognosis by capturing the real-world model of distinguishing one type of disease from another. Their studies show that several deep learning frameworks like VGG16 with 95.11%, ResNet50 at 93.88%, and the proposed IVX16 model yielded the highest accuracy of 96.94%. Furthermore, the adoption of transfer learning in the IVX16 model is an example of the following state-of-the-art trend, in which using pre-trained networks helps to achieve high results even when the amount of data collected in healthcare is limited. Notably, the integration of Explainable AI into their workflow not only confirms the predictions formulated by the model but also solves the most significant problem of transparency in AI-based healthcare solutions. Such advancements show that DL could significantly transform BTC and detection, keep patients from adverse outcomes, and respect ethical data use <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref29" id="context_ref_29_2" data-range="ref29">[29]</a>.</p><p>To what extent these two methods can improve the precision of BT image processing was the focus of the study conducted by Reddy and his group in 2024. The proposed approaches tested the research using MRI images of the tumor origin acquired from different types of BT; BWT and ROBPCA samples consisted of 16. Having completed image segmentation, the researchers derived textural features required for tumor work. Specifically, the test was mostly centered around just how accurate and sensitive or insensitive it was. It used a 1:1 enrolment ratio, a significance level of 5%, a desired power of 80%, and a confidence level of 95%. It was found that there was no difference between the two groups that were significant at the level of 0.05 (F =2.493; p =0.182). Combined, the sensitivity was 81.5%, and specificity was 84%, with the ROBPCA method being superior to BWT in the detection and analysis of tumor regions <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref28" id="context_ref_28_2" data-range="ref28">[28]</a>. A CNN model was developed by Das et al. <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref30" id="context_ref_30_2" data-range="ref30">[30]</a>, which has the ability to diagnose brain tumors in T1-weighted contrast-enhanced MRI images. These clears show why it is crucial to enhance the picture quality before the classification process is done. The authors of the paper worked with a dataset of the number of 3,064 pictures. The experiments of the CNN model were highly successful, with an average accuracy of 94.39 %, average precision of 93.33 %, and average recall of 93%. A similar performance suggests that the proposed model improves the diagnosis of brain tumors over current approaches due to the ability of CNNs to enhance classification tasks in the context of medical images <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref30" id="context_ref_30_2" data-range="ref30">[30]</a>.</p></div>
    <div class="section" id="sec3"><div class="header article-hdr"><div class="kicker">
                                SECTION III.</div><h2>Problem Statement</h2></div><p>Medical imaging, like MRI studies, is used to classify brain tumors. This is an important part of diagnosing and planning treatments for patients. Traditional machine learning models require centralized datasets, which can be challenging to achieve due to data privacy concerns, particularly in the medical field where patient data is highly sensitive. Hospitals and medical institutions often have isolated, decentralized datasets that cannot be easily shared, limiting the potential for collaborative learning from a larger, more diverse set of data. The goal is to use FL to make a strong BTC system that protects privacy. The main goal of this work is to use the FedAvg and FedProx methods to let many institutions train a DL model together without sharing their data. The research looks into how to quickly train a model that can sort four different kinds of brain conditions using data from different clients, taking into account the fact that these clients have different amounts of data. This work aims to address the following key challenges:
</p><ol><li value="1" data-list-item-label="1."><p><b>Data Privacy</b>: Developing a system that allows collaboration without sharing sensitive medical data across institutions.</p></li><li value="2" data-list-item-label="2."><p><b>Client Heterogeneity</b>: Handling differences in data distribution, size, and computational power across clients by incorporating the FedProx proximal term into the training process.</p></li><li value="3" data-list-item-label="3."><p><b>Model Performance</b>: Achieving high accuracy, precision, recall, and F1-score in classifying brain tumors while training models in a decentralized environment.</p></li></ol><p></p></div>
    <div class="section" id="sec4"><div class="header article-hdr"><div class="kicker">
                                SECTION IV.</div><h2>Proposed Methodology</h2></div><p><a ref-type="fig" anchor="fig2" class="fulltext-link">Figure 2</a> shows the proposed methodology and the process flow.</p><div class="section_2" id="sec4a"><h3>A. Dataset Description</h3><p>This BTC task uses the Brain Tumor MRI Dataset from Kaggle, which is a combination of figshare, SARTAJ, and Br35H dataset images with 4 classifications such as gliomas, meningiomas, pituitary tumors, and no tumors. For model training and evaluation, the dataset is separated into training and testing subsets. In the training portion, 1,321 gliomas, 1,339 meningiomas, 1,457 pituitary tumors, and 1,595 no tumor photos make up 5,712 images. The testing subset includes 1,311 images: 300 gliomas, 306 meningiomas, 300 pituitary tumors, and 405 no tumors <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref31" id="context_ref_31_4a" data-range="ref31">[31]</a>. The dataset provides a balanced and comprehensive representation of brain tumor types and normal brain scans, ensuring the model has sufficient data to learn the distinguishing features between each class. The inclusion of both train and test data enables the evaluation of the modelâ€™s generalization capability. The variety in the dataset allows for detailed performance analysis, particularly in FL scenarios, where data from multiple clients or sources can be aggregated while maintaining data privacy. This dataset is ideal for building a robust multi-class classification model capable of accurately diagnosing different types of BT from MRI images.</p></div><div class="section_2" id="sec4b"><h3>B. Load and Pre-Process Data</h3><p class="has-inline-formula">To begin, load the MRI dataset of brain tumors. To make sure that the input dimensions are consistent, every image is shrunk to a standard size of <inline-formula class="inline-formula"><tex-math notation="LaTeX"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style="display: none;"></span><span class="MathJax" id="MathJax-Element-1-Frame" tabindex="0" style=""><nobr><span class="math" id="MathJax-Span-1" style="width: 5.272em; display: inline-block;"><span style="display: inline-block; position: relative; width: 4.247em; height: 0px; font-size: 124%;"><span style="position: absolute; clip: rect(1.431em, 1004.2em, 2.441em, -1000em); top: -2.258em; left: 0em;"><span class="mrow" id="MathJax-Span-2"><span class="mn" id="MathJax-Span-3" style="font-family: MathJax_Main;">128</span><span class="mo" id="MathJax-Span-4" style="font-family: MathJax_Main; padding-left: 0.222em;">Ã—</span><span class="mn" id="MathJax-Span-5" style="font-family: MathJax_Main; padding-left: 0.222em;">128</span></span><span style="display: inline-block; width: 0px; height: 2.258em;"></span></span></span><span style="display: inline-block; overflow: hidden; vertical-align: -0.094em; border-left: 0px solid; width: 0px; height: 0.986em;"></span></span></nobr></span>
</tex-math></inline-formula> pixels. Pixel values are normalized by scaling them to the range [0,1] in order to make model training more efficient. Additionally, class labels are converted to one-hot encoded vectors, enabling the model to perform multi-class classification. For instance, the class label for glioma is represented as [1,0,0,0] for a four-class problem.</p></div><div class="section_2" id="sec4c"><h3>C. Split Data Among Clients</h3><p>For the purpose of simulating an FL scenario, the dataset is divided into K parts, where K is the number of clients that are taking part in the training process. This split should ensure that each client receives approximately the same number of data, helping to maintain balance in the training distribution. This process mimics real-world scenarios where data is often distributed across multiple clients, such as hospitals or institutions.</p></div><div class="section_2" id="sec4d"><h3>D. CNN Model</h3><p>A CNN is defined to classify the BT images. The architecture typically includes several convolutional layers followed by pooling layers and ReLU activation functions to introduce non-linearity. The final layer is a softmax layer that outputs probabilities for the four categories, enabling the model to make predictions for the given input images</p></div><div class="section_2" id="sec4e"><h3>E. Define Fedprox Loss</h3><p class="has-inline-formula">The FedProx loss function is designed to address the heterogeneity among clients in FL. It comprises two components: The local loss, which is computed using categorical cross-entropy:<disp-formula id="" class="display-formula"><tex-math notation="LaTeX"><span class="MathJax_Preview" style="display: none;"></span><div class="MathJax_Display" style="text-align: center;"><span class="MathJax" id="MathJax-Element-2-Frame" tabindex="0" style="text-align: center;"><nobr><span class="math" id="MathJax-Span-6" style="width: 19.089em; display: inline-block;"><span style="display: inline-block; position: relative; width: 15.376em; height: 0px; font-size: 124%;"><span style="position: absolute; clip: rect(0.403em, 1015.22em, 3.823em, -1000em); top: -2.312em; left: 0em;"><span class="mrow" id="MathJax-Span-7"><span class="msubsup" id="MathJax-Span-8"><span style="display: inline-block; position: relative; width: 2.579em; height: 0px;"><span style="position: absolute; clip: rect(3.114em, 1000.75em, 4.157em, -1000em); top: -3.978em; left: 0em;"><span class="texatom" id="MathJax-Span-9"><span class="mrow" id="MathJax-Span-10"><span class="mi" id="MathJax-Span-11" style="font-family: MathJax_Caligraphic-bold;">L</span></span></span><span style="display: inline-block; width: 0px; height: 3.978em;"></span></span><span style="position: absolute; top: -3.828em; left: 0.788em;"><span class="texatom" id="MathJax-Span-12"><span class="mrow" id="MathJax-Span-13"><span class="mi" id="MathJax-Span-14" style="font-size: 70.7%; font-family: MathJax_Math-bold-italic;">l</span><span class="mi" id="MathJax-Span-15" style="font-size: 70.7%; font-family: MathJax_Math-bold-italic;">o</span><span class="mi" id="MathJax-Span-16" style="font-size: 70.7%; font-family: MathJax_Math-bold-italic;">c</span><span class="mi" id="MathJax-Span-17" style="font-size: 70.7%; font-family: MathJax_Math-bold-italic;">a</span><span class="mi" id="MathJax-Span-18" style="font-size: 70.7%; font-family: MathJax_Math-bold-italic;">l</span></span></span><span style="display: inline-block; width: 0px; height: 3.978em;"></span></span></span></span><span class="mrow" id="MathJax-Span-19" style="padding-left: 0.167em;"><span class="mo" id="MathJax-Span-20" style="font-family: MathJax_Main;">(</span><span class="texatom" id="MathJax-Span-21"><span class="mrow" id="MathJax-Span-22"><span class="texatom" id="MathJax-Span-23"><span class="mrow" id="MathJax-Span-24"><span class="msubsup" id="MathJax-Span-25"><span style="display: inline-block; position: relative; width: 1.299em; height: 0px;"><span style="p