3.067em, 1006.07em, 4.433em, -1000em); top: -3.978em; left: 0em;"><span class="mrow" id="MathJax-Span-1024"><span class="mo" id="MathJax-Span-1025" style=""><span style="font-family: MathJax_Main;">âˆ¥</span></span><span class="texatom" id="MathJax-Span-1026"><span class="mrow" id="MathJax-Span-1027"><span class="texatom" id="MathJax-Span-1028"><span class="mrow" id="MathJax-Span-1029"><span class="msubsup" id="MathJax-Span-1030"><span style="display: inline-block; position: relative; width: 1.079em; height: 0px;"><span style="position: absolute; clip: rect(3.364em, 1000.69em, 4.148em, -1000em); top: -3.978em; left: 0em;"><span class="mi" id="MathJax-Span-1031" style="font-family: MathJax_Math-bold-italic;">Ï‰</span><span style="display: inline-block; width: 0px; height: 3.978em;"></span></span><span style="position: absolute; top: -3.828em; left: 0.718em;"><span class="texatom" id="MathJax-Span-1032"><span class="mrow" id="MathJax-Span-1033"><span class="mi" id="MathJax-Span-1034" style="font-size: 70.7%; font-family: MathJax_Math-bold-italic;">i</span></span></span><span style="display: inline-block; width: 0px; height: 3.978em;"></span></span></span></span><span class="mo" id="MathJax-Span-1035" style="font-family: MathJax_Main; padding-left: 0.222em;">âˆ’</span><span class="msubsup" id="MathJax-Span-1036" style="padding-left: 0.222em;"><span style="display: inline-block; position: relative; width: 2.9em; height: 0px;"><span style="position: absolute; clip: rect(3.364em, 1000.69em, 4.148em, -1000em); top: -3.978em; left: 0em;"><span class="mi" id="MathJax-Span-1037" style="font-family: MathJax_Math-bold-italic;">Ï‰</span><span style="display: inline-block; width: 0px; height: 3.978em;"></span></span><span style="position: absolute; top: -3.828em; left: 0.718em;"><span class="texatom" id="MathJax-Span-1038"><span class="mrow" id="MathJax-Span-1039"><span class="mi" id="MathJax-Span-1040" style="font-size: 70.7%; font-family: MathJax_Math-bold-italic;">g</span><span class="mi" id="MathJax-Span-1041" style="font-size: 70.7%; font-family: MathJax_Math-bold-italic;">l</span><span class="mi" id="MathJax-Span-1042" style="font-size: 70.7%; font-family: MathJax_Math-bold-italic;">o</span><span class="mi" id="MathJax-Span-1043" style="font-size: 70.7%; font-family: MathJax_Math-bold-italic;">b</span><span class="mi" id="MathJax-Span-1044" style="font-size: 70.7%; font-family: MathJax_Math-bold-italic;">a</span><span class="mi" id="MathJax-Span-1045" style="font-size: 70.7%; font-family: MathJax_Math-bold-italic;">l</span></span></span><span style="display: inline-block; width: 0px; height: 3.978em;"></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class="mo" id="MathJax-Span-1046" style=""><span style="font-family: MathJax_Main;">âˆ¥</span></span></span><span style="display: inline-block; width: 0px; height: 3.978em;"></span></span><span style="position: absolute; top: -4.455em; left: 6.202em;"><span class="texatom" id="MathJax-Span-1047"><span class="mrow" id="MathJax-Span-1048"><span class="mn" id="MathJax-Span-1049" style="font-size: 70.7%; font-family: MathJax_Main-bold;">2</span></span></span><span style="display: inline-block; width: 0px; height: 3.978em;"></span></span></span></span></span><span style="display: inline-block; width: 0px; height: 2.204em;"></span></span></span><span style="display: inline-block; overflow: hidden; vertical-align: -0.53em; border-left: 0px solid; width: 0px; height: 1.761em;"></span></span></nobr></span>
</tex-math></inline-formula> ensures that the local model updates do not diverge too much from the global model, thereby stabilizing training in non-IID environments. This is particularly important in brain tumor classification tasks where MRI scans may be significantly different between clients, yet the global model must still be generalized effectively. By constraining the local models, FedProx helps the global model converge more robustly, mitigating the risk of poor performance due to client data heterogeneity.</p></div></div>
    <div class="section" id="sec5"><div class="header article-hdr"><div class="kicker">
                                SECTION V.</div><h2>Result and Discussion</h2></div><div class="section_2" id="sec5a"><h3>A. Global Model Accuracy</h3><p>The model performance for Round 1 is 0.8899, which is a reasonable start. It tells that the model for the assignment is quite good as it is. It was observed that the accuracy rate remains relatively stable across Rounds 2 to Round 4, but it continues to rise gradually to 0.9176 in Round 4.</p><p>The accuracy at Round 5 jumps to 0.9413 to indicate that the model has been trained. Starting from Round 6 to Round 10, the accuracy rises again as much as in Round 10, with an accuracy of 0.9719. In general, there is a systematic improvement in the accuracy over the rounds, especially in the first five rounds. The following rounds do not present a significant growth, which is quite typical since the model is close to its final conclusions as shown in <a ref-type="fig" anchor="fig5" class="fulltext-link">figure 5</a>.</p></div><div class="section_2" id="sec5b"><h3>B. Global Precision</h3><p>As shown in <a ref-type="fig" anchor="fig6" class="fulltext-link">figure 6</a>, the precision of this system is 0.8186 in Round 1, which is an indication of a fairly large number of false positives. In Round 2, precision is 0.8788and it is quite visible that the results have become better. The precision increases gradually round by round, standing at 0.9164 in Round 4 and 0.9406 in Round 5. The precision of the system is at its highest at 0.9725 in Round 10, meaning that the system has been able to minimize the false positives. This is evidenced by the rising precision as the model trains as it becomes spot on in predicting positive cases while making fewer wrong decisions.</p></div><div class="section_2" id="sec5c"><h3>C. Global Recall</h3><p>The recall begins at 0.8162 in the first round and is almost equivalent to the precision value, which shows the modelâ€™s ability to classify positives without generating too many false alarms as shown in <a ref-type="fig" anchor="fig7" class="fulltext-link">figure 7</a>. From Round 2 to Round 4, the recall increases gradually and gets to 0.9176 in Round 4. Recall capacity also remains on the rise and rises dramatically to 0.9504 in Round 6 and then reaches its highest level at 0.9719 by Round 10. This is evidence that the model is improving at recognizing the true positives and not compromising the ability to avoid false negatives.
</p><div class="figure figure-full" id="fig7"><!--
          Workaround for combined images.Eg.- 1000116 Fig. 5
        --><div class="img-wrap"><a href="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/mediastore/IEEE/content/media/6287639/10820123/10918716/tejan7-3549440-large.gif" data-fig-id="fig7"><img class="document-ft-image" src="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/mediastore/IEEE/content/media/6287639/10820123/10918716/tejan7-3549440-small.gif" loading="lazy" alt="FIGURE 7. - Global model recall."></a><div class="zoom-container"><button class="zoom" title="View Larger Image" aria-label="View Larger Image" data-fig-id="fig7"></button></div></div><div class="figcaption"><b class="title">FIGURE 7. </b><fig><p>Global model recall.</p></fig></div><p class="links"><button ref-type="fig-link" data-docid="10918716" class="all">Show All</button></p></div><p></p></div><div class="section_2" id="sec5d"><h3>D. Global F1 Score</h3><p>In <a ref-type="fig" anchor="fig8" class="fulltext-link">figure 8</a>, the F1 score comes to 0.8111 in Round 1, and it is slightly lower than the precision and recall but quite close to the modeâ€™s initial performance metrics. The F1 score for Round 2 is 0.8759, which is an increase from the previous round. Thus, by Round 5, the F1 score has risen to 0.9408 and is evidence of good performance both in terms of true positive prediction and minimal false positives. The F1 score keeps on rising, reaching 0.9718 in Round 10, this shows that the model is well equipped to balance between precision and recall. The F1 score gives a similar pattern to the other metrics, with a steep increase at round 5 and then a slow and steady rise till the end. This implies that at the later rounds, the model is not only increasing its efficiency in identifying the positive cases but also reducing the wrong identification<b>.</b>:
</p><div class="figure figure-full" id="fig8"><!--
          Workaround for combined images.Eg.- 1000116 Fig. 5
        --><div class="img-wrap"><a href="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/mediastore/IEEE/content/media/6287639/10820123/10918716/tejan8-3549440-large.gif" data-fig-id="fig8"><img class="document-ft-image" src="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/mediastore/IEEE/content/media/6287639/10820123/10918716/tejan8-3549440-small.gif" loading="lazy" alt="FIGURE 8. - Global mode F1 score."></a><div class="zoom-container"><button class="zoom" title="View Larger Image" aria-label="View Larger Image" data-fig-id="fig8"></button></div></div><div class="figcaption"><b class="title">FIGURE 8. </b><fig><p>Global mode F1 score.</p></fig></div><p class="links"><button ref-type="fig-link" data-docid="10918716" class="all">Show All</button></p></div><p></p><p><a ref-type="table" anchor="table4" class="fulltext-link">Table 4</a> presents the progression of a global modelâ€™s performance over 10 rounds of training or evaluation, with the key metrics values. Letâ€™s break down and analyze each round and the trends observed for these metrics.</p><div class="figure figure-full table" id="table4"><div class="figcaption"><b class="title">TABLE 4 </b>Global Model Evaluation Metrics Across Rounds</div><div class="img-wrap"><a href="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/mediastore/IEEE/content/media/6287639/10820123/10918716/tejan.t4-3549440-large.gif"><img class="document-ft-image" src="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/mediastore/IEEE/content/media/6287639/10820123/10918716/tejan.t4-3549440-small.gif" loading="lazy" alt="Table 4- Global Model Evaluation Metrics Across Rounds"></a><div class="zoom-container"><button class="zoom" title="View Larger Image" aria-label="View Larger Image" data-type="table" data-src="/mediastore/IEEE/content/media/6287639/10820123/10918716/tejan.t4-3549440-large.gif" data-fig-id="table4"></button></div></div></div><p></p></div><div class="section_2" id="sec5e"><h3>E. Analysis and Observation</h3><p>
          </p><ol><li value="1" data-list-item-label="1."><p><i>Steady Improvement Across All Metrics:</i> The trend for all the key metrics is upward, with even more dramatic increases in the early rounds. There is a significant change in the model by Round 5 from Round 1, with a further marginal increase up to Round 10. So, this tells that the model is learning good.</p></li><li value="2" data-list-item-label="2."><p><i>Precision-Recall Balance:</i> Both the precision and recall values are quite similar for all the rounds, which means that the model does not predict many false positive or false negative cases. This is important, especially in tasks such as medical image classification, where both false negatives and false positives are expensive.</p></li><li value="3" data-list-item-label="3."><p><i>Significant Jump in Mid-Rounds:</i> We can see a marked improvement in the performance between Round 4 and Round 5 in terms of accuracy and F1 score. This could suggest that the model has arrived at some critical point at which the generalization capability was enhanced significantly. From Round 5 onwards, the improvements are not much, this is an indication that the model is converging gradually.</p></li><li value="4" data-list-item-label="4."><p>Convergence and Saturation: At Round 8, the trends of the accuracy and F1 score become relatively stable, and the increases are insignificant between Rounds 9 and 10. This is expected as the model maximizes in a way that additional training may not necessarily lead to the highest result. This means it has become quite stable at the end of Round 10 with accuracy and F1 performance of more than 0.97.</p></li></ol>
        <p></p></div></div>
    <div class="section" id="sec6"><div class="header article-hdr"><div class="kicker">
                                SECTION VI.</div><h2>Comparative Analysis</h2></div><p>The <a ref-type="table" anchor="table5" class="fulltext-link">table 5</a> shows various models for the classification of brain tumors with their accuracies. The proposed analysis starts with BWT and ROBPCA, the method used by Chappidi Sree Teja Reddy et al. in 2024. The BWT and ROBPCA obtained accuracies of 81.50% and 84%, respectively. These reduced models are easy to implement but may fail in more challenging imaging situations, which shows the need for more elaborate methods in tumor categorization.</p><div class="figure figure-full table" id="table5"><div class="figcaption"><b class="title">TABLE 5 </b>Accuracy Comparison</div><div class="img-wrap"><a href="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/mediastore/IEEE/content/media/6287639/10820123/10918716/tejan.t5-3549440-large.gif"><img class="document-ft-image" src="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/mediastore/IEEE/content/media/6287639/10820123/10918716/tejan.t5-3549440-small.gif" loading="lazy" alt="Table 5- Accuracy Comparison"></a><div class="zoom-container"><button class="zoom" title="View Larger Image" aria-label="View Larger Image" data-type="table" data-src="/mediastore/IEEE/content/media/6287639/10820123/10918716/tejan.t5-3549440-large.gif" data-fig-id="table5"></button></div></div></div><p></p><p>Ullah et al. <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref26" id="context_ref_26_6" data-range="ref26">[26]</a> used Gabor filters along with ResNet50 and SVM, where the accuracy of the system was found to be 95.73%, respectively. On the other hand, this hybrid method takes advantage of the feature extraction property of DL models, but at the same time, it adds computational complexity. Ravinder et al. <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref27" id="context_ref_27_6" data-range="ref27">[27]</a> then improved this with a Graph-based Convolutional Neural Network (GCNN) which yielded an accuracy of 95.01%. The GCNN model is an extension of GNN and CNN, for non-Euclidean data, but it is expensive in terms of training. Hossain et al. <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref29" id="context_ref_29_6" data-range="ref29">[29]</a> have come up with a new model with an accuracy of 96.94%. This approach showed that using deep learning methods for classification is viable and efficient even though the size of the dataset remains a problem that affects most deep learning applications. Das et al. <a ref-type="bibr" anchor="ref30" id="context_ref_30_6" data-range="ref30">[30]</a> used a basic CNN model, which gave an accuracy of 94.39%.</p><p>Liu D et al. proposed the G-ResNet model which achieved 95% accuracy and demonstrated capabilities of deep learning based classification using residual networks. Ge C et al. also created a Multi-stream 2D-CNN model with 88.82% accuracy that utilized feature extraction with multiple input streams. Afshar P et al. had lower accuracy with 73.90% while implementing BayesCap, a model aimed at capturing prediction uncertainty, highlighting issues of uncertainty quantification for classification. Cheng Y et al. on the other hand used the ConvCaps model and reported an accuracy of 93.50%, illustrating the effectiveness of capsule networks in retaining spatial hierarchies. Further, Abiwiananda N et al. built a Custom CNN model that had 84.19% accuracy, showcasing flexibility of architectures based on CNNs. Naser M A et al. performed well with VGG16, a deep convolutional network, and reported an accuracy of 89%. S. Bhadauriya et al. reported using CNN integrated with Federated Learning (FL) and reported a high accuracy of 96% showing the effectiveness of these new learning methods.</p><p>As shown in the <a ref-type="fig" anchor="fig9" class="fulltext-link">figure 9</a>, the FedAvg+FedProx model proposed in this work achieved an accuracy of 97.19%, which is the highest accuracy compared with other models presented in this work or in the literature. FL Unlike the other architectures, FedAvg+FedProx uses a decentralized learning approach to address the challenge of client cooperation through model training without transferring data. For overall model training, FedAvg+FedProx approaches data heterogeneity in clients, which can be hospitals or medical centers, by using the FedProx algorithm, which makes the training process stable even for clients with very distinct data distribution. This enhancement is of particular value as the model can be applied across a wide range of sources of data and is, therefore, stronger than other models based on a single source of data.
</p><div class="figure figure-full" id="fig9"><!--
          Workaround for combined images.Eg.- 1000116 Fig. 5
        --><div class="img-wrap"><a href="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/mediastore/IEEE/content/media/6287639/10820123/10918716/tejan9-3549440-large.gif" data-fig-id="fig9"><img class="document-ft-image" src="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/mediastore/IEEE/content/media/6287639/10820123/10918716/tejan9-3549440-small.gif" loading="lazy" alt="FIGURE 9. - Accuracy comparison of proposed model."></a><div class="zoom-container"><button class="zoom" title="View Larger Image" aria-label="View Larger Image" data-fig-id="fig9"></button></div></div><div class="figcaption"><b class="title">FIGURE 9. </b><fig><p>Accuracy comparison of proposed model.</p></fig></div><p class="links"><button ref-type="fig-link" data-docid="10918716" class="all">Show All</button></p></div><p></p></div>
    <div class="section" id="sec7"><div class="header article-hdr"><div class="kicker">
                                SECTION VII.</div><h2>Limitations and Future Work</h2></div><p>This study carries with it some limitations, such as using a highly specific and constrained database offered by Kaggle which does not represent real life variations in MRI scans, patient demographic, and imaging procedures, thus affecting generalizability. Furthermore, the scope is still an issue since the FL framework was implemented with a small group of clients compared to what would be found in practice at various healthcare institutions that differ in terms of network penetration and hardware availability. Implementing FL, particularly with CNN based models, also incurs significant computation and communication expenses which, as is known, are rather difficult to meet in resource constrained environments. And finally, the model is clinically has not been applied against actual hospital data, which is one of the crucial steps towards making it reliable in real life medical practices.</p><p>The future work would be attempting to lessen the communication and computation overload that is present utilizing model compression methods along with optimized communication techniques that would increase the feasibility of implementing FL-based CNN models in a constrained environment. Moreover, clinical validation and real-life testing are necessary steps that must be done to assess how the model would perform with real hospital data, making it more generalizable to varying patient populations and imaging conditions, so as to enhance its reliability. An integrated approach using other modalities alongside MRI scans such as clinical notes or case history of the patient can increase the diagnostic accuracy through the use of complementary data and improve it. Also, the use of explainable AI (XAI) will increase the level of understanding of the medical imaging decision-making model so that it becomes easy for the medical professionals to accept and trust its outcomes, which in turn makes it possible to meet the legal and ethical responsibilities of using AI in medical imaging.</p></div>
    <div class="section" id="sec8"><div class="header article-hdr"><div class="kicker">
                                SECTION VIII.</div><h2>Conclusion</h2></div><p>This BTC work implemented using FL shows major improvement in the model performance and its ability to generalize with the help of the Proposed Fed+FedPorx approach. The work then overcomes the challenges of data privacy, heterogeneity, and distributed learning by extending and combining FedAvg and FedProx. The Fed+FedPorx model proposed in this paper is better than conventional architectures. The employment of FedAvg helps in the formation of updates from the participating clients to the server reducing learning bias and improving the learning process while at the same time maintaining the privacy of the patientâ€™s data. However, FedProx adds a proximal term to leverage the modelâ€™s capacity to learn from data that is not Independent and Identically Distributed (non-IID) across different clients.</p><p>The Proposed Fed+FedPorx model achieved 97.19% accuracy and 97.18% F1 score, which confirms the success of FL in the medical imaging domain. This approach not only enhances the classification performance but also facilitates better clinical decisions through accurate and private prediction. Altogether, the results of this work indicate that FL can be applied successfully for improvements of the brain tumor classification systems using FedAvg and FedProx algorithms. This study presents a foundation for future works and real implementations in medical imaging, thus opening new possibilities for improving the existing and developing new mechanisms of Machine Learning in the healthcare sector.</p></div>
