<div id="recommendations-layout" class="recommendations-layout" data-tracking-area="recommendations-layout"><div class="post-list" style="height: auto; visibility: visible;"><div class="recommendations-wrapper" style="width: 100%;"><div id="recommend-main-c9" class="recommend-main  "><section id="col-organic-c21" class="col-organic"><header id="recommendations-header" class="recommend-col-header"><h2 class="recommendations-unit-title">Also on <strong class="recommendations-forum">IEEE Xplore</strong></h2></header><div class="recommendations-unit"><div class="recommendation-container"><button class="scroll-btn scroll-left" data-action="scroll-left" tabindex="-1">â®</button><div class="recommend-wrapper"><div class="recommend-posts" data-role="recommend-posts"><div class="recommend-post post-0" id="recommend-link-organic-10609674532" data-role="recommended-post" data-link="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/11142272" tabindex="0"><header class="recommend-post-header"><div class="recommend-image-wrapper"><img src="https://c.disquscdn.com/get?url=https%3A%2F%2Fieeexplore.ieee.org%2Fielx8%2F6287639%2F10820123%2F11142272%2Fgraphical_abstract%2Faccess-gagraphic-3602975.jpg&amp;key=uFLnxJHuM5oNkwoVYeaOMQ&amp;h=200" alt="Clarke-Park Transformation-Based Input Configuration for CNN Diagnosis of Stator Faults in Three Phase Induction Motors Using Motor Current Signature Analysis" title="Clarke-Park Transformation-Based Input Configuration for CNN Diagnosis of Stator Faults in Three Phase Induction Motors Using Motor Current Signature Analysis"><span class="overlay"></span></div><h3 class="recommend-post-title" title="Clarke-Park Transformation-Based Input Configuration for CNN Diagnosis of Stator Faults in Three Phase Induction Motors Using Motor Current Signature Analysis"><span data-role="recommend-thread-title" class="title line-truncate" data-line-truncate="3" data-content="Clarke-Park Transformation-Based Input Configuration for CNN Diagnosis of Stator Faults in Three Phase Induction Motors Using Motor Current Signature Analysis">Clarke-Park Transformation-Based Input â€¦</span></h3></header><ul class="meta" style="bottom: 104px;"><li class="time">a year ago</li> <li class="comments">1 comment </li></ul><div class="content-preview-wrapper"><div class="recommend-content"><span data-role="recommend-description-snippet" class="line-truncate" data-line-truncate="5">Motor current signature analysis (MCSA) with convolutional neural networks (CNNs) is widely applied to three-phase induction motor fault diagnosis. Conventional input configurations, such as direct three-phase RGB mapping or discrete wavelet transform (DWT), primarily depend on inter-phase amplitude and phase differences, limiting their ability to capture subtle physical responses, especially for inter-turn short circuits (ITSC). This study proposes a Clarkeâ€“Park transformation (CPT)-based input configuration that extracts &lt;inline-formula xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"&gt; &lt;tex-math notation="LaTeX"&gt;$I_{\alpha }$ &lt;/tex-math&gt;&lt;/inline-formula&gt;,&lt;inline-formula xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"&gt; &lt;tex-math notation="LaTeX"&gt;$I_{\beta }$ &lt;/tex-math&gt;&lt;/inline-formula&gt; and &lt;inline-formula xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"&gt; &lt;tex-math notation="LaTeX"&gt;$I_{d}$ &lt;/tex-math&gt;&lt;/inline-formula&gt; components and assigns them to the red, green, and blue channels, respectively. This design simulatneously represents spatial current distribution (&lt;inline-formula xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"&gt; &lt;tex-math notation="LaTeX"&gt;$I_{\alpha }$ &lt;/tex-math&gt;&lt;/inline-formula&gt;,&lt;inline-formula xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"&gt; &lt;tex-math notation="LaTeX"&gt;$I_{\beta }$ &lt;/tex-math&gt;&lt;/inline-formula&gt;) and dynamic response (&lt;inline-formula xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"&gt; &lt;tex-math notation="LaTeX"&gt;$I_{d}$ &lt;/tex-math&gt;&lt;/inline-formula&gt;) in the rotating reference frame, enhancing physical interpretability and discriminative capability. Comparative experiments using identical raw data and the ResNet50 model evaluated CPT against RGB mapping and DWT inputs. CPT achieved the highest test accuracy (99.51%), with notable consistency in the challenging ITSC class (F1-score: 0.993) and perfect classification of the 4Turn class. Grad-CAM visualizations confirmed that CPT produces more localized, fault-focused activations than RGB mapping, comparable to DWT, without requiring complex frequency transforms. These results demonstrate that CPT effectively organizes current signal features, enabling faster convergence, improved classification stability, and high diagnostic accuracy, offering a physically meaningful input design for reliable motor fault diagnosis.</span></div></div></div><div class="recommend-post post-1" id="recommend-link-organic-10613733020" data-role="recommended-post" data-link="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/11146764" tabindex="0"><header class="recommend-post-header"><div class="recommend-image-wrapper"><img src="https://c.disquscdn.com/get?url=https%3A%2F%2Fieeexplore.ieee.org%2Fielx8%2F6287639%2F10820123%2F11146764%2Fgraphical_abstract%2Faccess-gagraphic-3605175.jpg&amp;key=HcaHDq762CgLWgW6FKuJaQ&amp;h=200" alt="Hybrid Quantumâ€“Classical Framework for Urban Traffic State Classification" title="Hybrid Quantumâ€“Classical Framework for Urban Traffic State Classification"><span class="overlay"></span></div><h3 class="recommend-post-title" title="Hybrid Quantumâ€“Classical Framework for Urban Traffic State Classification"><span data-role="recommend-thread-title" class="title line-truncate" data-line-truncate="3" data-content="Hybrid Quantumâ€“Classical Framework for Urban Traffic State Classification">Hybrid Quantumâ€“Classical Framework for â€¦</span></h3></header><ul class="meta" style="bottom: 104px;"><li class="time">a year ago</li> <li class="comments">1 comment </li></ul><div class="content-preview-wrapper"><div class="recommend-content"><span data-role="recommend-description-snippet" class="line-truncate" data-line-truncate="5">This paper presents a hybrid quantumâ€“classical approach for multiclass traffic state classification using a benchmark urban mobility dataset collected in Daejeon, South Korea. The proposed architecture, called the Multilayer Perceptronâ€“Variational Quantum Classifier (MVQC), combines classical feature extraction via a multilayer perceptron (MLP) with quantum-based classification using a variational quantum classifier (VQC). On the medium-scale subset of the dataset, the MVQC model achieved a test accuracy of 91.4%, outperforming the standalone VQC model, which achieved 61.8%. Although MVQC did not reach the accuracy of the classical MLP model (98.6%), it consistently outperformed purely quantum models and demonstrated better scalability with increasing data complexity. All quantum operations were simulated under noise-free conditions using IBM Qiskitâ€™s statevector simulator. These results demonstrate that classical feature extraction can effectively guide quantum circuits, enhance learning performance, and highlight the hybrid MVQC architecture as a viable solution for time-series classification in intelligent transportation systems.</span></div></div></div><div class="recommend-post post-2" id="recommend-link-organic-10670419624" data-role="recommended-post" data-link="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/11205344" tabindex="0"><header class="recommend-post-header"><div class="recommend-image-wrapper"><img src="https://c.disquscdn.com/get?url=https%3A%2F%2Fieeexplore.ieee.org%2Fielx8%2F6287639%2F10820123%2F11205344%2Fgraphical_abstract%2Faccess-gagraphic-3622325.jpg&amp;key=4S478ZZLeKlcU81LlHt_Tg&amp;h=200" alt="Streaming Event-Triggered Point-on-Wave Measurements for Disturbance Monitoring and Analysis" title="Streaming Event-Triggered Point-on-Wave Measurements for Disturbance Monitoring and Analysis"><span class="overlay"></span></div><h3 class="recommend-post-title" title="Streaming Event-Triggered Point-on-Wave Measurements for Disturbance Monitoring and Analysis"><span data-role="recommend-thread-title" class="title line-truncate" data-line-truncate="3" data-content="Streaming Event-Triggered Point-on-Wave Measurements for Disturbance Monitoring and Analysis">Streaming Event-Triggered Point-on-Wave â€¦</span></h3></header><ul class="meta" style="bottom: 104px;"><li class="time">a year ago</li> <li class="comments">1 comment </li></ul><div class="content-preview-wrapper"><div class="recommend-content"><span data-role="recommend-description-snippet" class="line-truncate" data-line-truncate="5">Phasor measurements become unreliable during power system disturbances due to nonsinusoidal waveform distortions, particularly in grids with high penetration of inverter-based resources (IBRs). To address this, the North American Electric Reliability Corporation (NERC) recommends the use of high-resolution point-on-wave (POW) measurements for enhanced monitoring of IBR-related events. However, the existing infrastructure at most utilities is not equipped to support continuous POW data streaming from the grid edge to control centers. To overcome this challenge, this paper proposes an automated, condition-based framework that triggers short-duration POW recording and streaming over the existing phasor measurement unit (PMU) communication infrastructure. The triggering mechanism is governed by a goodness-of-fit (GOF) error metric that quantifies the mismatch between raw waveform data and its estimated phasor model. The proposed approach employs a dual-streaming strategy, in which phasor data is streamed continuously, while POW data is transmitted intermittently during disturbance events when the GOF error exceeds a predefined threshold. The framework leverages the flexible Streaming Telemetry Transport Protocol (STTP) to enable dual-mode data streaming. In this process, POW disturbance records are segmented into serialized, timestamped packets and inserted into idle intervals between successive phasor transmissions over a shared communication channel. The bandwidth performance of the proposed approach is evaluated through a case study, demonstrating that a 0.1-second POW disturbance can be streamed with up to 98% channel utilization over a 4-second interval using only 20% bandwidth headroom on the PMU infrastructure. A proof-of-concept implementation in MATLAB/Simulink validates the proposed framework using both synthetic and field-measured waveform data.</span></div></div></div><div class="recommend-post post-3" id="recommend-link-organic-10622294686" data-role="recommended-post" data-link="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/11164708" tabindex="0"><header class="recommend-post-header"><div class="recommend-image-wrapper"><img src="https://c.disquscdn.com/get?url=https%3A%2F%2Fieeexplore.ieee.org%2Fielx8%2F6287639%2F10820123%2F11164708%2Fgraphical_abstract%2Faccess-gagraphic-3610168.jpg&amp;key=oc4CoKQnAa8aN5HtuvAkpA&amp;h=200" alt="Development of a Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM)-Based Statistical Model for Earthquake Forecasting in Central Asia" title="Development of a Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM)-Based Statistical Model for Earthquake Forecasting in Central Asia"><span class="overlay"></span></div><h3 class="recommend-post-title" title="Development of a Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM)-Based Statistical Model for Earthquake Forecasting in Central Asia"><span data-role="recommend-thread-title" class="title line-truncate" data-line-truncate="3" data-content="Development of a Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM)-Based Statistical Model for Earthquake Forecasting in Central Asia">Development of a Long Short-Term Memory â€¦</span></h3></header><ul class="meta" style="bottom: 104px;"><li class="time">a year ago</li> <li class="comments">1 comment </li></ul><div class="content-preview-wrapper"><div class="recommend-content"><span data-role="recommend-description-snippet" class="line-truncate" data-line-truncate="5">Earthquake forecasting using traditional methods remains a complex task due to the inherent nonlinearity and stochastic nature of seismic activity. Therefore, this study examines the application of deep learning methods, particularly a hybrid model combining Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) and Convolutional Neural Network (CNN), to create a robust prediction model that outperforms traditional methods. The study comprises six primary phases: data acquisition, pre-processing, exploratory analysis, seismicity characterization, model implementation, and effectiveness assessment. The dataset contained 17,565 earthquake events (&lt;inline-formula xmlns:mml="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"&gt; &lt;tex-math notation="LaTeX"&gt;$M \geq 3.0$ &lt;/tex-math&gt;&lt;/inline-formula&gt;) in Central Asia, collected from 1973â€“2024 via the United States Geological Survey (USGS) Application Programming Interface (API), enriched with geological features such as distance to active faults (from Global Earthquake Model (GEM) datasets) and eight key seismicity indicators. Compared with baseline architectures (standard LSTM and fully connected neural network), the proposed CNN-LSTM hybrid achieved superior performance, particularly in recall (0.80 compared with 0.67) and F1-score (0.77 compared with 0.71), demonstrating its ability to capture both spatial and temporal dependencies. The results indicate that using a hybrid CNN-LSTM model leads to a significant improvement in forecasting strong earthquakes. This is validated by a Receiver Operating Characteristicâ€“Area Under the Curve (ROC-AUC) of 0.78 and an F1-score of 0.77, supporting a good balance between model completeness and accuracy. These improvements confirm that the regional adaptation of CNN-LSTM, combined with seismic indicators and clustering-based subregionalization, provides a novel methodological contribution to earthquake forecasting in Central Asia. The model reliably distinguishes periods of elevated seismic risk from quiet intervals, supporting practical applications in regional forecasting and seismic risk visualization.</span></div></div></div><div class="recommend-post post-4" id="recommend-link-organic-10807620834" data-role="recommended-post" data-link="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/11278166" tabindex="0"><header class="recommend-post-header"><div class="recommend-image-wrapper"><img src="https://c.disquscdn.com/get?url=https%3A%2F%2Fieeexplore.ieee.org%2Fielx8%2F6287639%2F10820123%2F11278166%2Fgraphical_abstract%2Faccess-gagraphic-3640507.jpg&amp;key=jnWdFNrs6O4newBHQq9T5A&amp;h=200" alt="SW3T-IoT: Smart Water Monitoring Based on a Three-Tier IoT Architecture" title="SW3T-IoT: Smart Water Monitoring Based on a Three-Tier IoT Architecture"><span class="overlay"></span></div><h3 class="recommend-post-title" title="SW3T-IoT: Smart Water Monitoring Based on a Three-Tier IoT Architecture"><span data-role="recommend-thread-title" class="title line-truncate" data-line-truncate="3" data-content="SW3T-IoT: Smart Water Monitoring Based on a Three-Tier IoT Architecture">SW3T-IoT: Smart Water Monitoring Based on a â€¦</span></h3></header><ul class="meta" style="bottom: 104px;"><li class="time">10 months ago</li> <li class="comments">1 comment </li></ul><div class="content-preview-wrapper"><div class="recommend-content"><span data-role="recommend-description-snippet" class="line-truncate" data-line-truncate="5">The growing impacts of climate change on water resources demand innovative monitoring and management strategies. This paper presents SW3T-IoT, an innovative water monitoring system leveraging Internet of Things (IoT) technologies to enhance water resource management under dynamic conditions. The system adopts a three-tier architectureâ€”edge, fog, and cloudâ€”integrating a custom-designed LoRa-WDS node optimized for low power consumption and long-term autonomous operation. These nodes collect real-time data on water level, flow, pressure, temperature, and quality from distribution networks and reservoirs. Data are locally processed at the edge, then transferred to the cloud for advanced analysis and visualization. A Geographic Information System (GIS) enhances spatial awareness and decision-making. To ensure adaptability, SW3T-IoT integrates a continual learning (CL) framework for Biochemical Oxygen Demand (BOD) forecasting. The CL mechanism allows models to incrementally update with new environmental data without catastrophic forgetting, maintaining accuracy and robustness as water quality dynamics evolve. A hybrid CNNâ€“BiLSTMâ€“Attention model, reinforced with PER, AER, LwF, and EWC strategies, supports adaptive prediction and knowledge retention. The system delivers intuitive dashboards for engineers and water managers, providing a holistic, scalable, and intelligent solution for sustainable water resource management.</span></div></div></div><div class="recommend-post post-5" id="recommend-link-organic-10932389836" data-role="recommended-post" data-link="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/11397330" tabindex="0"><header class="recommend-post-header"><div class="recommend-image-wrapper"><img src="https://c.disquscdn.com/get?url=https%3A%2F%2Fieeexplore.ieee.org%2Fielx8%2F6287639%2F11323511%2F11397330%2Fgraphical_abstract%2Faccess-gagraphic-3665213.jpg&amp;key=yQrFrb4yUrk5XgO0oeKwVQ&amp;h=200" alt="Verification of Novel SAR Measurement System Utilizing Multi-Probe Planar Approach Inversion Algorithm" title="Verification of Novel SAR Measurement System Utilizing Multi-Probe Planar Approach Inversion Algorithm"><span class="overlay"></span></div><h3 class="recommend-post-title" title="Verification of Novel SAR Measurement System Utilizing Multi-Probe Planar Approach Inversion Algorithm"><span data-role="recommend-thread-title" class="title line-truncate" data-line-truncate="3" data-content="Verification of Novel SAR Measurement System Utilizing Multi-Probe Planar Approach Inversion Algorithm">Verification of Novel SAR Measurement â€¦</span></h3></header><ul class="meta" style="bottom: 104px;"><li class="time">7 months ago</li> <li class="comments">1 comment </li></ul><div class="content-preview-wrapper"><div class="recommend-content"><span data-role="recommend-description-snippet" class="line-truncate" data-line-truncate="5">The certification of wireless devices necessitates rapid and precise Specific Absorption Rate (SAR) assessment, a critical yet challenging requirement in modern electromagnetic compatibility (EMC) testing. Conventional systems, dependent on sequential mechanical scanning, are fundamentally limited by low throughput and inherent probe-induced field distortions, creating a bottleneck for large-scale certification. This paper presents a novel multi-probe planar inversion system (0.6â€“6 GHz) designed to overcome these limitations. The key to this proposed system is the development and integration of a dedicated 3D field inversion algorithm within a novel system framework for correcting probe-induced errors. This algorithm uniquely integrates experimental and simulated data to correct systematic probe errors, enabling high-fidelity SAR reconstruction with a maximum deviation of less than 3% from the reference standard. Through experimental validation, the proposed system demonstrates a reduced measurement time, from several hours to just minutes. By simultaneously resolving the critical trade-off between speed and accuracy, this work provides a robust and efficient framework that is directly applicable to accelerating the EMC compliance pipeline for 5G, Internet of Things (IoT), and next-generation wireless technologies.</span></div></div></div><div class="recommend-post post-6" id="recommend-link-organic-11197574936" data-role="recommended-post" data-link="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/11550057" tabindex="0"><header class="recommend-post-header"><div class="recommend-image-wrapper"><img src="https://c.disquscdn.com/get?url=https%3A%2F%2Fieeexplore.ieee.org%2Fassets%2Fimg%2Fieee_logo_smedia_200X200.png&amp;key=8mkBk-DK1FemHl2uWrZfPg&amp;h=200" alt="Coordinated Planning of Collaborative Interconnected Microgrids under Intermittent DERs and Dynamic Loads using Geese V-Formation Algorithm (GVFA)" title="Coordinated Planning of Collaborative Interconnected Microgrids under Intermittent DERs and Dynamic Loads using Geese V-Formation Algorithm (GVFA)"><span class="overlay"></span></div><h3 class="recommend-post-title" title="Coordinated Planning of Collaborative Interconnected Microgrids under Intermittent DERs and Dynamic Loads using Geese V-Formation Algorithm (GVFA)"><span data-role="recommend-thread-title" class="title line-truncate" data-line-truncate="3" data-content="Coordinated Planning of Collaborative Interconnected Microgrids under Intermittent DERs and Dynamic Loads using Geese V-Formation Algorithm (GVFA)">Coordinated Planning of Collaborative â€¦</span></h3></header><ul class="meta" style="bottom: 104px;"><li class="time">4 months ago</li> <li class="comments">1 comment </li></ul><div class="content-preview-wrapper"><div class="recommend-content"><span data-role="recommend-description-snippet" class="line-truncate" data-line-truncate="5">Interconnected microgrids (IMGs) offer opportunities to aggregate distributed energy resources (DERs) for loss reduction and voltage improvement, yet planning under daily variability remains challenging. This study presents a physics-aware planning and validation framework for five interconnected IEEE-33 microgrids integrating photovoltaic (PV), battery energy storage (BESS), one microgrid-specific Distributed Energy Resources (wind, hydro, biogas, or diesel), Electric Vehicle charging demand, and shunt capacitors (CAP). Snapshot optimization was performed using the Geese V-Formation Algorithm with a loss-focused objective, adequacy, and undervoltage penalties. The optimized configuration was validated over 24 hours using an AC backwardâ€“forward sweep under dynamic loads, weather-based Distributed Energy Resources generation, and Battery Energy Storage Systems state-of-charge constraints. The final GVFA planning simulation, implemented in Python, demonstrated feeder loss reductions of 50â€“70% and minimum voltage improvements between 0.956 and 0.966 p.u., with higher values during hourly operation. Three microgrids consistently acted as net-exporters (island-capable), while two remained net-importers, leveraging tie-line diversity. Methodologically, Geese V-Formation Algorithmâ€™s leaderâ€“follower dynamics, neighbor-local coupling, and stochastic rounding efficiently traversed a mixed-integer, nonconvex search space. Systemically, the collaborative interconnected approach delivered system-wide benefits beyond localized optimization. The framework provides actionable recommendations for Distributed Energy Resources siting and sizing to enhance loss reduction, voltage stability, and operational flexibility under realistic intermittent conditions.</span></div></div></div><div class="recommend-post post-7" id="recommend-link-organic-10876401911" data-role="recommended-post" data-link="https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/11343780" tabindex="0"><header class="recommend-post-header"><div class="recommend-image-wrapper"><img src="https://c.disquscdn.com/get?url=https%3A%2F%2Fieeexplore.ieee.org%2Fassets%2Fimg%2Fieee_logo_smedia_200X200.png&amp;key=8mkBk-DK1FemHl2uWrZfPg&amp;h=200" alt="EvoAVX: Island-Based Genetic Algorithm Library with AVX-512 and MPI" title="EvoAVX: Island-Based Genetic Algorithm Library with AVX-512 and MPI"><span class="overlay"></span></div><h3 class="recommend-post-title" title="EvoAVX: Island-Based Genetic Algorithm Library with AVX-512 and MPI"><span data-role="recommend-thread-title" class="title line-truncate" data-line-truncate="3" data-content="EvoAVX: Island-Based Genetic Algorithm Library with AVX-512 and MPI">EvoAVX: Island-Based Genetic Algorithm â€¦</span></h3></header><ul class="meta" style="bottom: 104px;"><li class="time">8 months ago</li> <li class="comments">1 comment </li></ul><div class="content-preview-wrapper"><div class="recommend-content"><span data-role="recommend-description-snippet" class="line-truncate" data-line-truncate="5">Genetic algorithms are practical tools for solving complex optimization problems; however, the performance of many existing solutions is often limited by their inability to utilize modern CPU capabilities fully. This paper introduces EvoAVX, an open-source island-based genetic algorithm library engineered for minimal computational overhead. EvoAVX addresses the lack of specialized, high-performance libraries by leveraging AVX-512 intrinsics to optimize genetic operators, statistics calculations, and the pseudorandom number generator. The library provides a built-in, selectable set of vectorized operators. Static polymorphism is implemented via C++ templates to ensure fast, compile-time operator resolution. EvoAVX employs a double-precision real-valued gene representation. The Island model is built on the Message Passing Interface, which enables distributed computing. Our performance evaluation shows notable improvements in execution time compared to popular libraries for evolutionary computation. The performance advantages of EvoAVX are particularly pronounced in scenarios where the fitness evaluation is computationally inexpensive, making it an ideal tool for high-throughput optimization tasks.</span></div></div></div></div></div><button class="scroll-btn scroll-right" data-action="scroll-right" tabindex="-1">â¯</button></div></div></section><